💡 Single Channel ADC LED Control

Project Description:
This STM32 project demonstrates how to read analog values using a single ADC channel and control an LED based on the value.

If the ADC value < 2048, the LED is OFF.

If the ADC value ≥ 2048, the LED is ON.

This is a beginner-friendly project to understand ADC reading, conditional logic, and GPIO control on STM32.

🔹 How It Works

Connect an analog input (e.g., potentiometer) to an ADC pin of STM32.

STM32 reads the analog voltage and converts it into a digital value (0–4095) using the ADC.

Compare the ADC value with 2048 to decide the LED state:

if(adcResult < 2048) {
    HAL_GPIO_WritePin(LED_GPIO_Port, LED_Pin, GPIO_PIN_RESET); // LED OFF
} else {
    HAL_GPIO_WritePin(LED_GPIO_Port, LED_Pin, GPIO_PIN_SET);   // LED ON
}

🔹 Explanation of ADC HAL Functions
HAL_ADC_Start(&hadc1);
HAL_ADC_PollForConversion(&hadc1, 100);
adcResult = HAL_ADC_GetValue(&hadc1);
HAL_ADC_Stop(&hadc1);


HAL_ADC_Start(&hadc1);

Starts the ADC conversion process on the configured channel.

HAL_ADC_PollForConversion(&hadc1, 100);

Waits (polls) until the ADC conversion is complete.

100 is the timeout in milliseconds.

adcResult = HAL_ADC_GetValue(&hadc1);

Reads the converted digital value from the ADC.

Value range depends on ADC resolution (e.g., 0–4095 for 12-bit).

HAL_ADC_Stop(&hadc1);

Stops the ADC to free resources.

🔹 Features

Learn single-channel ADC reading

